"executive_summary": "This paper addresses the high computational complexity and suboptimal performance of existing hybrid precoding schemes in millimeter-wave (mmWave) massive MIMO systems. The authors propose a novel framework that integrates deep learning (DNN) to optimize the selection of hybrid precoders. By treating the precoder selection as a mapping relation within a deep neural network, the study aims to exploit the spatial sparsity of mmWave channels more effectively than traditional mathematical methods like Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) or Geometric Mean Decomposition (GMD). The system model utilizes a Saleh-Valenzuela channel model with a uniform linear array at the base station. Extensive simulations were conducted using the Keras framework with a carrier frequency of 28 GHz and 3 non-line-of-sight components. The results demonstrate that the proposed DNN-based approach significantly outperforms conventional schemes in terms of Bit Error Ratio (BER) and spectrum efficiency. Furthermore, the method achieves superior convergence speed, requiring approximately half the iterations of analog precoding schemes to reach the Mean Square Error (MSE) target. The study concludes that deep learning offers a robust solution for reducing hardware complexity while enhancing system throughput in next-generation communications.",
